Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 07:58:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Build KasaFlow behavioural evaluator (task #217)

Webb, 2026-09-01: stop adding isolated fixes, freeze the transaction
architecture, and prove the whole conversational system against a
structured evaluation suite before the next architectural change.

- scripts/evaluator/schema.py: Scenario/Turn/CheckResult/ScenarioResult
  dataclasses. A Turn checks whatever subset of expected_tool,
  expected_fields, expected_contains, expected_not_contains it
  specifies. Correctness and manner are deliberately separate axes --
  manner_note is carried into the report for a human (or a future
  LLM-judge pass) to score; this evaluator does not fabricate an
  automated manner verdict for something it cannot actually judge.
- scripts/evaluator/runner.py: runs a scenario's turns through the
  real route_customer() pipeline, sharing one session across turns,
  and reads router.py's own KASAFLOW_TURN_TRACE log line (built
  2026-08-21) as its data source rather than re-deriving
  llm_structured_output/resolved_arguments a second time.
- scripts/evaluator/scenarios.py: 46 scenarios across all fifteen
  categories Webb specified. Every scenario's source states plainly
  whether it is an exact case lifted from this repo's existing test
  script, task history, or live traces (confirmed live, dated) or
  newly designed from the category spec and not yet live-tested --
  these are not presented as equally verified. Includes Webb's own
  end-to-end golden-path journey as references-04.
- scripts/run_evaluator.py: CLI (--category/--id/--json), exits
  non-zero on any failure.
- tests/test_evaluator.py: 17 tests against the harness mechanics
  (trace capture, comparison logic, corpus well-formedness), all with
  route_customer() mocked -- no real API key needed for these. Full
  suite: 720 passed, 29 skipped.

Not done here, and cannot be done in this sandbox: an actual scored
run needs a real OPENAI_API_KEY. The corpus is a starting point Webb
asked for explicitly, not another batch of manually chosen questions,
built to be extended as real failures turn up.
